{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And Aaron and his sons shall wash from it their hands and their feet.</b> He would sanctify his feet and his hands at once. And we learned in Zevaḥim: How does the priest sanctify? He places his right hand upon his right foot and his left hand upon his left foot and sanctifies." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): the reason for washing the hands is from the protocols of royalty, for such is the way of attendants who draw near to the king's table — to wash their hands first, because the hands are busy instruments; and the washing of the feet, because they would minister barefoot." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: the reason for washing the hands and the feet alludes to the ten sefirot, for the first of a man is ten when he raises his hands on high and his last is ten. And this is the reason that our Rabbis of blessed memory ordained for us the taking of hands (נטילת ידים) in prayer; and the term נטילה alludes to this, for it is a term of lifting, in the manner of (Psalms 134:2) ״שאו ידיכם קדש״, and this is (Psalms 119) ״ואשא כפי אל מצותיך אשר אהבתי״ and so forth. And therefore Onkelos translated the word ורחצו with a term of sanctification, saying ויקדשון, and did not translate ויסחון as is his practice in other places, to allude that the priest who ministers clothes himself and is sanctified in the sanctity of the ten sefirot. And in the form of man there is a likeness to them: ten fingers of the hands with the covenant of the tongue between them, and likewise ten toes of the feet with the covenant of circumcision between them."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:05:45.844008678Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}